concepts.content:
The **inverse Fourier transform** relation is defined as: 

$$\ell\left[n,m ight] = \mathcal{F}^{-1} \left\{ \mathscr{L}\left[u,v ight] ight\} =  rac{1}{NM} \sum_{u=0}^{N-1} \sum_{v=0}^{M-1} \mathscr{L}\left[u,v ight] \exp{ \left(+2\pi j \left( rac{u\, n}{N} +  rac{v\, m}{M} ight) ight) } $${#eq-inversefourier}

As seen from the inverse transform equation, the image is rewritten as a sum of complex exponentials rather than a sum of offset pixel values. Each exponential is at a different frequency, called a spatial frequency for images because they describe how quickly things vary across space. To construct an image from a Fourier transform $\mathscr{L}\left[u,v ight]$, we add in the corresponding amount of that particular complex exponential (conjugated).

